[[:db/retract 17592186134059 :question/answer-fields 17592186130510] {:db/id 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" "question-a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" "question-03426578-7377-543b-bd88-93276fa901ef" "question-6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/id #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/math-academy-id "q-104637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer-field/choices ["answer-376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" "answer-db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" "answer-75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" "answer-1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" "answer-87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/id #uuid "db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/id #uuid "75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/id #uuid "1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/id #uuid "87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" :question/id #uuid "a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" :question/math-academy-id "q-104639" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{3})-(0\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" "answer-2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" "answer-b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" "answer-d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" "answer-cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" :answer/id #uuid "2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" :answer/id #uuid "b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" :answer/id #uuid "d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10" :answer/id #uuid "cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-03426578-7377-543b-bd88-93276fa901ef" :question/id #uuid "03426578-7377-543b-bd88-93276fa901ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-104638" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,{t}^{2}-1,{t}^{2}-2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨5,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,-1,0)$ and $(5,3,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(5,3,0)-f(1,-1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{5(0)}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5aa64add-60f3-564a-9626-a2687efca2f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5aa64add-60f3-564a-9626-a2687efca2f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa64add-60f3-564a-9626-a2687efca2f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47d59bce-11d1-59ca-a980-8662920cfd55" "answer-d992fc1d-6425-5951-9f5f-b0d131ab532a" "answer-cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" "answer-e72ad3e5-d9a9-5e06-b65e-7fe892762e86" "answer-10c8a508-1d4a-574f-bb45-6efb1ced71fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47d59bce-11d1-59ca-a980-8662920cfd55" :answer/id #uuid "47d59bce-11d1-59ca-a980-8662920cfd55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d992fc1d-6425-5951-9f5f-b0d131ab532a" :answer/id #uuid "d992fc1d-6425-5951-9f5f-b0d131ab532a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer/id #uuid "cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e72ad3e5-d9a9-5e06-b65e-7fe892762e86" :answer/id #uuid "e72ad3e5-d9a9-5e06-b65e-7fe892762e86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{e}^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10c8a508-1d4a-574f-bb45-6efb1ced71fc" :answer/id #uuid "10c8a508-1d4a-574f-bb45-6efb1ced71fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac" :question/id #uuid "6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104624" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =(3+{4}^{2})-(-1+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3+16)-(-1) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" "answer-12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" "answer-cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" "answer-48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" "answer-b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" :answer/id #uuid "a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" :answer/id #uuid "12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/id #uuid "cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" :answer/id #uuid "48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b" :answer/id #uuid "b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134059 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{3}+1)-({0}^{3}+0) \\\\  & =(27+1)-0 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d412271-dad8-50a6-a5b4-2d3051bb421a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d412271-dad8-50a6-a5b4-2d3051bb421a" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d412271-dad8-50a6-a5b4-2d3051bb421a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2214f1f-3631-5be2-85e4-d5102a1c44c0" "answer-d6de32bd-280a-5c5a-aa34-19f1b3faadf0" "answer-5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" "answer-1aeef040-2421-5b1c-b55a-b92ee77dadcc" "answer-00980506-979a-5a3c-bb6b-3b50f009bfac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2214f1f-3631-5be2-85e4-d5102a1c44c0" :answer/id #uuid "a2214f1f-3631-5be2-85e4-d5102a1c44c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6de32bd-280a-5c5a-aa34-19f1b3faadf0" :answer/id #uuid "d6de32bd-280a-5c5a-aa34-19f1b3faadf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer/id #uuid "5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aeef040-2421-5b1c-b55a-b92ee77dadcc" :answer/id #uuid "1aeef040-2421-5b1c-b55a-b92ee77dadcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00980506-979a-5a3c-bb6b-3b50f009bfac" :answer/id #uuid "00980506-979a-5a3c-bb6b-3b50f009bfac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70" :question/id #uuid "9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-104732" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t,\\frac{\\pi t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y)\\,i-({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c9c0c78-b891-5f32-8dca-852634763b9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c9c0c78-b891-5f32-8dca-852634763b9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9c0c78-b891-5f32-8dca-852634763b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" "answer-29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" "answer-62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" "answer-aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" "answer-c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" :answer/id #uuid "b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" :answer/id #uuid "62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" :answer/id #uuid "aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba" :answer/id #uuid "c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135084 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nA similar result, known as **the fundamental theorem for line integrals,** exists for line integrals of conservative vector fields. This theorem can be stated as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ Then,*\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll discuss how this theorem is proved in simple cases at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's take a look at a concrete example of how to apply this theorem." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135128 :tutorial/content "Here, we prove the fundamental theorem of line integrals in the two-dimensional case. The three-dimensional case is similar.\n\nLet $C$ be a smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ We wish to show that\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nUsing our usual formula for calculating line integrals, we can write the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}∇f\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the integrand above is simply the derivative of $f(\\mathbf r(t))$ with respect to $t.$ Thus, we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(f(\\mathbf r(t)))\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, this is equal to \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nThis concludes the proof in the case where $C$ is smooth. It's easy to show that the theorem also holds for piecewise smooth curves." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133996 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
